CLAIM_HE25.check:
The Brain has not extracted the same substantive claim twice from this paper. Yes = it is a distinct contention; No = it duplicates another CLM of this source (give that id in the comment).

The defect being assessed is duplication BY THE BRAIN, not repetition by the paper:
• duplication is judged within one source only;
• the paper repeating a proposition in its own prose is irrelevant — the system should extract that proposition once;
• an equivalent claim made by a different paper is NOT a failure here: those remain separate claim nodes.

CLAIM_HE05.check:
The force of the proposition is preserved. What matters is the strength asserted, not the exact words used. Three broad categories:

• reality / assertion — is, reports a finding;
• possibility / qualification — may, can, suggests;
• requirement / normativity — should, ought, must.

A defect is movement between these categories, for example: possibility becoming certainty; a suggestion becoming a demonstrated finding; a recommendation becoming a factual description. Harmless lexical variation within the same category — one expression of a requirement replaced by another — is NOT a defect.

CLAIM_HE06.check:
The anchors show where in the source the claim is expressed. They are a provenance and anti-hallucination device.

• anchors are verbatim passages from the source;
• together they must ground the material components of the claim;
• they need NOT reproduce the whole reasoning or the full evidentiary basis for the claim — that is not what anchors are for;
• they must not stitch unrelated passages into a proposition the source never makes.

Do not fail an anchor set merely because the argument supporting the claim is longer than the quoted text.

CLAIM_HE20.check:
A proposition can be connected to an arbitrarily large set of broader, narrower, implicit and downstream concepts. This criterion is bounded by the purpose of the artifact — a systematic mapping review.

Do NOT require:
• every concept that could logically be inferred;
• every broader or narrower concept;
• every concept associated with a technique merely because that technique could be used for the task.

DO require concepts whose omission would materially impair how this claim is represented in the mapping. The question is not "could I connect this claim to another concept?" but "is a materially important concept missing?"
